[recv] {"method":"item/completed","params":{"item":{"type":"agentMessage","id":"msg_0da5d1257be04eee016a2071fb76c881939ecb2dc6410447b5","text":"Implemented the minimal RP2040 breakout in [index.circuit.tsx](/workspace/index.circuit.tsx).\n\nWhat’s included:\n- RP2040 QFN56 model with direct pin labels\n- USB-C connector\n- USB D+/D- series resistors and CC pulldowns\n- 3.3 V LDO section\n- SPI flash\n- 12 MHz crystal as an explicit two-pad footprint\n- LED on GP25\n- Two 8-pin breakout headers\n- No `@tsci/*` registry component imports\n- No `routingDisabled`\n\nGenerated outputs are in `/workspace/dist/index/`:\n- `circuit.json`\n- `pcb.svg`\n- `schematic.svg`\n- `3d.png`\n\nValidation run:\n- `npm run typecheck`: passed\n- `tsci check netlist index.circuit.tsx`: 0 errors, 0 warnings\n- `tsci check placement index.circuit.tsx`: 0 errors, 0 warnings\n- `tsci build`: exits successfully, but reports DRC/routing-quality errors from explicit straight rail segments and supplier-footprint mismatch warnings on generic passives.\n\n**TSCircuit Developer Feedback**\n- Confusing/surprising: `pcbStraightLine` only works for exactly two physical ports, not named nets, and that error was only clear after trying it.\n- Most time wasted: debugging autorouter failures where the failed node name like `cmn_2` was not mapped back to a readable net in CLI output.\n- Helpful changes: CLI should report failed autorouter nodes as readable net/component names and distinguish fatal build failures from DRC warnings more clearly.\n- Specific friction: built-in `crystal_3225` footprint string was accepted enough to proceed but later failed as an invalid footprint; USB-C built-in pin names and physical model also produced non-obvious routing/DRC behavior.","phase":"final_answer","memoryCitation":null},"threadId":"019e8ead-3fd5-7af3-b65a-79c9bbea0956","turnId":"019e8ead-3fee-7b92-8527-f198eb42614b","completedAtMs":1780511234746}}
